    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **28 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field derived from an owner's city using the fixed city-to-region table (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD, else UNKNOWN).\n\nFollowing the codebase's existing pattern for derived owner fields (`displayName`, `initials`, `membershipNumber`, `membershipTier`), locality is a `readOnly`, purely-derived output field \u2014 no DB column needed. Three changes:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `readOnly` `locality` property to the `Owner` schema, so the code-generated `OwnerDto` exposes the field.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerLocality.java`** (new) \u2014 A small static helper holding the fixed city-to-region lookup, returning `\"UNKNOWN\"` for any unlisted (or null) city.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a `@Mapping` expression populating `locality` from `owner.getCity()` via `OwnerLocality.of(...)`.\n\n### One notable pitfall\nMy first attempt put the lookup as a `default String locality(String city)` method on the mapper interface. MapStruct treats any `String \u2192 String` method on a mapper as an **implicit mapping method** and silently applied it to *other* string properties \u2014 it rewrote `telephone` to `\"UNKNOWN\"`, causing `InvalidTelephoneException` (400) across owner-creation tests. Moving the logic into a standalone helper class referenced by fully-qualified name in the expression avoids that ambiguity entirely, which is why `OwnerLocality` is a separate class rather than a mapper method.\n\nCp17's two new tests (known city \u2192 `NSW`, unknown city \u2192 `UNKNOWN`) pass, and all 26 pre-existing tests remain green.",
